---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import WildlifePhoto from '../components/WildlifePhoto.astro';
import { wildlifeSpecies, wildlifeFieldSources, wildlifeGuideFaqs } from '../data/wildlifeFieldGuide';
import { wildlifeDistanceGroups, wildlifeContacts, wildlifeReviewedAt, wildlifeSources } from '../data/wildlifeSafety';
---
<BaseLayout lastUpdated={wildlifeReviewedAt} title="Banff Wildlife Guide — Bears, Elk, Moose & Field Notes" description="Meet nine Banff wildlife species with identification clues, credited historical photographs, habitat context and responsible viewing plans. Learn before you explore.">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:wildlifeGuideFaqs.map(f=>({'@type':'Question',name:f.question,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text:f.answer}}))})} />
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Activities',href:'/activities'},{label:'Wildlife'}]} /></div>
  <section class="guide-hero"><div class="container hero-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Look closely. Leave room.</span><h1>Wildlife in Banff</h1><p>A pale rump in the forest. A sharp call from the rocks. The change from wetland to mountain slope. There is more to a wildlife day than finding a bear.</p><a href="/wildlife-tracker" class="btn btn-accent">Explore habitat and area plans →</a><p class="reviewed">Species and planning review: <time datetime={wildlifeReviewedAt}>29 October 2026</time></p></div><WildlifePhoto id="elk" eager /></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container intro"><h2>Make the landscape the destination</h2><p>Banff’s montane valleys, subalpine forests and alpine terrain support different kinds of wildlife. The town and Minnewanka area sit in the montane; Lake Louise is a useful example of subalpine forest. Choose a designated walk, scenic stop or viewpoint, then enjoy what the habitat reveals. <a href="https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/ab/banff/nature/faune-wildlife">Read Parks Canada’s habitat introduction</a>.</p><div class="guide-links"><a href="#field-guide">Identify nine species</a><a href="#field-notebook">Try the field notebook</a><a href="/wildlife-calendar">Plan for your month</a><a href="/wildlife-safety">Prepare for an encounter</a></div><p class="photo-note">Photographs below are historical species illustrations, with creator and licence credits. They are not current sightings or evidence of an animal at a mapped visitor area.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section field-section" id="field-guide"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><h2>Meet the neighbours</h2><p>Learn features before travelling. Use binoculars or a photograph from an appropriate distance; never approach to settle an identification.</p></div><div class="species-grid">{wildlifeSpecies.map(s=><article class="species-card" id={s.id}>{s.photo&&<WildlifePhoto id={s.photo} />}<div class="species-body"><h3>{s.name}</h3><p class="scientific"><i>{s.scientific}</i></p><h4>Notice</h4><p>{s.identify}</p><h4>Understand</h4><p>{s.ecology}</p><h4>Plan the view</h4><p>{s.viewing}</p><a href={s.source}>Identification and habitat source →</a></div></article>)}</div></div></section>
  <section class="section" id="field-notebook"><div class="container notebook"><span class="eyebrow">A little practice before the trail</span><h2>Your field notebook</h2><p>Think through each clue, then open the note. No animal needs to be found, fed or followed to complete this activity.</p><details><summary>A brown bear crosses a distant view. Does the colour identify its species?</summary><p>No. Black bears can have brown or cinnamon coats. Use several body and face features together, from a distance. If you encounter a bear, follow behaviour-based <a href="/wildlife-safety">Parks Canada encounter guidance</a>; a colour guess is not a response plan.</p><a href={wildlifeFieldSources.bearId}>Bear identification source</a></details><details><summary>White coat, beard and narrow black horns: sheep or goat?</summary><p>Those are mountain-goat clues. Bighorn sheep have brown coats; a ram’s horns curl heavily, while a ewe’s are shorter and narrower. Young, distant or partly hidden animals can be difficult to identify. Leave them room.</p><a href={wildlifeFieldSources.sheep}>Sheep and goat source</a></details><details><summary>A round-eared animal calls from a rock slide. What might it be?</summary><p>An American pika is one possibility. Pikas use talus and have no obvious tail. Listen and look from the designated route; do not climb after the call or disturb vegetation stored among rocks.</p><a href={wildlifeFieldSources.small}>Small-mammal source</a></details><details><summary>An elk stands on a path near town. Does the developed setting make passing close acceptable?</summary><p>No. Keep at least 30 metres from elk and add more space when needed. Wait well back or turn around instead of squeezing past. Spring calving and fall mating behaviour need particular caution.</p><a href={wildlifeSources.elk}>Elk safety source</a></details></div></section>
  <section class="section field-section"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><h2>A wildlife-aware day</h2><p>Start with space, preparation and an ordinary journey that works for the whole group.</p></div><div class="planning-grid"><div class="planning-card"><h3>1. Choose the outing</h3><p>Compare habitat, season and journey style in the <a href="/wildlife-tracker">area planner</a>. Read the actual route report and closure before leaving. A named viewing area does not promise a sighting or establish current access.</p></div><div class="planning-card"><h3>2. Pack to observe</h3><p>Bring binoculars, clothing and equipment for the chosen route, and accessible bear spray for trail travel. Learn its label and use before starting. Control food and keep dogs leashed.</p><a href="/bear-spray">Bear-spray purchase, rental and travel planning →</a></div><div class="planning-card"><h3>3. Give space</h3>{wildlifeDistanceGroups.map(g=><p><strong>{g.metres} metres minimum:</strong> {g.species.join(', ')}.</p>)}<p>Increase the distance if an animal reacts to you. Do not surround, lure, follow or crowd wildlife.</p><a href={wildlifeSources.viewing}>Parks Canada viewing guidance →</a></div><div class="planning-card"><h3>4. Leave well</h3><p>At roadside encounters, stay in your vehicle and keep moving. Only stop where safe and permitted, briefly and fully out of traffic. Leave if a wildlife jam develops. Pack away food and take all rubbish.</p><p>Report bear, cougar, wolf and coyote encounters once safe: <a href={wildlifeContacts.wildlifeReport.href}>{wildlifeContacts.wildlifeReport.phone}</a>. Emergency: <a href="tel:911">911</a>.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container tour-panel"><div><h2>Choose a guided landscape experience</h2><p>A naturalist can help explain habitat and responsible viewing. Check the actual itinerary, seasonal operation, transport and cancellation terms. Wildlife remains wild, and sightings are not guaranteed.</p><p class="affiliate-note">Affiliate links: BanffBound may earn a commission from qualifying bookings.</p></div><div class="tour-actions"><a href="https://www.getyourguide.com/banff-l284/?partner_id=QW960HO&q=wildlife" class="btn btn-accent" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">Compare guided wildlife tours →</a><a href="https://www.expedia.ca/Hotel-Search?destination=Banff%2C+Alberta&camref=1101l3MtWX" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">Find a Banff hotel →</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container notebook"><h2>Banff wildlife questions</h2>{wildlifeGuideFaqs.map(f=><details><summary>{f.question}</summary><p>{f.answer}</p></details>)}<p><a href="/wildlife-calendar">Continue with the seasonal preparation calendar →</a></p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.guide-hero{background:#173e2c;color:white;padding:3.5rem 0}.hero-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center}.guide-hero h1{color:white;font-size:clamp(2.4rem,5vw,3.8rem);margin:.7rem 0}.guide-hero p{line-height:1.75;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem}.eyebrow{text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.13em;font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700}.guide-hero .reviewed{font-size:.78rem;margin-top:1.3rem;opacity:.85}.intro{max-width:1000px}.intro>p{line-height:1.75;margin:1rem 0}.guide-links{display:flex;gap:.8rem 1.4rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:1.5rem 0}.guide-links a{font-weight:700}.photo-note{font-size:.83rem;color:#53685b}.field-section{background:var(--color-bg-alt)}#field-guide,#field-notebook{scroll-margin-top:110px}.species-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,300px),1fr));gap:1.5rem}.species-card{border-radius:14px;background:white;border:1px solid #d6e0d8;overflow:hidden}.species-body{padding:1.5rem}.species-body h3{font-size:1.4rem}.scientific{font-size:.8rem;color:#5b6e61;margin:.3rem 0 1rem}.species-body h4{font-size:.85rem;margin:1rem 0 .25rem}.species-body p:not(.scientific){line-height:1.7;font-size:.9rem}.species-body>a{display:block;margin-top:1rem;font-size:.8rem}.notebook{max-width:900px}.notebook>p{line-height:1.7;margin:1rem 0}.notebook details{padding:1.1rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d6e0d8}.notebook summary{font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.6}.notebook details p{line-height:1.75;margin:.8rem 0}.notebook details>a{font-size:.8rem}.planning-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:1.2rem}.planning-card{background:white;padding:1.5rem;border-radius:12px}.planning-card p{font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.75;margin:.7rem 0}.planning-card>a{font-size:.83rem}.tour-panel{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;gap:2rem;padding:2rem;border:1px solid #d6e0d8;border-radius:14px}.tour-panel p{line-height:1.7;margin:.8rem 0}.tour-actions{display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;gap:1rem}.affiliate-note{font-size:.75rem;color:#596b5e}@media(max-width:700px){.hero-grid,.tour-panel{grid-template-columns:1fr}.guide-hero{padding:2.5rem 0}.hero-grid{gap:1rem}.tour-panel{padding:1.3rem}}
</style>
